/*!
\page qtbridges-csharp-build-from-source.html
\title Building Qt Bridges C# module from source

\brief How to build the module and package it as a NuGet.

\section1 Building the NuGet Package and Test Application (Windows, .NET 8)

This guide documents the steps used to:

\list 1
\li Build a minimal Qt 6 from source (subset of modules)
\li Build the Qt.DotNet solution (adapter, generator, rules)
\li Pack a NuGet package
\li Build/Run the test application against the local package
\endlist

\note The paths below use \c{D:\work} for demonstration. Adjust as needed. All
commands are meant to run from \e{x64 Native Tools Command Prompt for VS 2022.}

\section2 Prerequisites

\list
\li Visual Studio 2022 (Desktop development with C++) with the x64 Native Tools
    Command Prompt
\li .NET SDK 8+ (\c{dotnet --version})
\li Git
\li CMake & ninja (installed with VS; available in the native tools prompt)
\li Python & Perl (required by Qt build tooling) - see Qt's system requirements
\li Sufficient disk space (Qt build can require tens of GB)
\endlist

\note System requirements reference: \l{https://wiki.qt.io/Building_Qt_6_from_Git#System_Requirements}

\section2 Build Qt 6 (subset) from source

Open \e{x64 Native Tools Command Prompt for VS 2022} and run:

\code
:: Choose a working directory
pushd D:\work

:: Create source/build/install folders
mkdir qt6-source
mkdir qt6-build
mkdir qt6-install

:: Clone Qt meta-repo (Qt6 uses the qt5 meta-repo name)
git clone git://code.qt.io/qt/qt5.git qt6-source

:: Initialize only the modules we need
cd qt6-source
init-repository --module-subset=qtbase,qtsvg,qtshadertools,qtdeclarative,qtquick3d,qtquick3dphysics,qtquicktimeline

:: Configure out-of-source build
cd ..\qt6-build
..\qt6-source\configure -prefix ..\qt6-install -release -opensource -confirm-license -submodules qtbase,qtsvg,qtshadertools,qtdeclarative,qtquick3d,qtquick3dphysics,qtquicktimeline -- -DQT_BUILD_TESTS=OFF -DQT_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF

:: Build and install
cmake --build .
cmake --install .
\endcode

\section3 Notes

\list
\li You can add \c{-D CMAKE_BUILD_PARALLEL_LEVEL=N} to speed up builds
    (or use \c{cmake --build . --parallel}).
\li If you run into generator issues, you can specify \c{-G "Ninja"} and install
    Ninja.
\li The above turns off Qt tests/examples to keep the build lean.
\endlist

\section2 Set Qt environment for the session

Use the \e{same} native tools prompt you used to build Qt:

\code
set QtInstallRoot=D:\work\qt6-install
\endcode

\section2 Build the Qt.DotNet solution

From the \e{same} prompt:

\code
:: Go to your Qt.DotNet source checkout
pushd D:\work\qtdotnet

:: Restore and build (Release)
dotnet build -c Release
\endcode

Build the test app as a smoke test:

\code
:: Build the test app (adjust the path if different)
dotnet build -c Release examples\Primes\Primes.csproj
\endcode

\section2 Test the package locally with the sample app

From the \e{same} prompt:

\code
:: Build & run the sample app
dotnet run --project examples\Primes\Primes.csproj -c Release
\endcode

\section2 Publish to nuget.org (optional)

\code
:: Push the package
set NUGET_API_KEY=***your key***
dotnet nuget push nuget\local\*.nupkg --api-key %NUGET_API_KEY% --source https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json
\endcode

\section2 Troubleshooting

\list
\li \b{MSVC/CMake not detected}: Make sure you're using the \e{x64 Native Tools}
    prompt.
\li \b{Missing Python/Perl}: Install them and ensure they're on \c{PATH} before
    running \c{init-repository}/\c{configure}.
\li \b{Long paths or spaces}: Prefer short, space-free paths like \c{D:\work}.
\li \b{Rebuild from scratch}: Delete the entire content of \c{qt6-build} and
    \c{qt6-install} directory and configure again.
\endlist

\section2 What gets packaged

The NuGet contains:

\list
\li .NET adapter (host Qt/QML engine from C#)
\li Generator (discovers your types and emits interop glue)
\li Filtering rules (Include/Ignore/Exclude attributes)
\li C++ include headers for the native bridge
\li A minimal open-source Qt Quick runtime subset sufficient to run QML
\endlist

All parts are versioned together to ensure compatibility.

\section2 Clean up

To revert environment changes, close the prompt or manually unset variables:

\code
set QtInstallRoot=
\endcode

After completing the steps above, you'll have:

\list
\li A locally built Qt install at \c{%QtInstallRoot%}
\li A Release build of Qt.DotNet
\li A NuGet package in \c{nuget\local}
\li A test application built against that package
\endlist

*/
